From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] iommu: sun60i: add the Allwinner A733 IOMMU driver

The display engine driver (`drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` and `drm-a733-kms-drm-device-for-the-display-pipe`) runs with a 32-bit DMA mask and the
board has 8 GiB of RAM. A GPU buffer above 4 GiB is bounced through swiotlb
when labwc imports it, and a large one fails outright: measured with glmark2
in a window, 256 swiotlb_bounced events for display-engine and "swiotlb
buffer is full (sz: 405504 bytes)". That mask
is the driver's choice, not the hardware's: the DE's layers have a
high-address byte (VI +0x0d0..+0x0e4, which `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` does not program).
The SoC has two IOMMUs that put any page behind a 32-bit IOVA; behind
IOMMU1 a scanout buffer is one IOVA range over scattered pages, and an
address outside its mappings faults instead of reaching memory. This driver
makes them usable through the IOMMU core; `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-iommu-gates-and-resets` to `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-behind-iommu1` wire IOMMU1 to the
display engine.

THE SAME CODE AS A v7.3-rc4 VERSION -- revised in place 2026-09-22

The driver, its Kconfig entry and the binding are also kept as a
two-patch series against v7.3-rc4, which has not been posted; this patch
carries the same code so that the board proof of this tree covers it.
drivers/iommu/sun60i-iommu.c differs from that series' file after `git am`
in two places: attach_dev() has no `old` argument on 6.18 (v7.3 added
it; the driver never reads it), so the last parameter line of the two
attach ops differs; and the series' resume_early is not in this
patch -- this tree adds it with the next one,
iommu-sun60i-program-the-block-again-on-resume, which also moves the
block writes of sun60i_iommu_master_translate() into
sun60i_iommu_master_program(). The master port gate (the
revision of 2026-09-24 below) is in the series since its regeneration of
2026-09-25. Everything else is byte for byte the series' file.
kzalloc_obj(*p, GFP_KERNEL) is valid on both: 6.18.36 takes the GFP
argument as required, v7.3 as optional (default_gfp()), and both expand to
__alloc_objs(kzalloc, GFP_KERNEL, typeof(P), 1).

What changed against the first revision, which is the one HW-proven on
2026-09-18:

  * identifiers a733_*/A733_* are sun60i_*/SUN60I_*, as upstream's A733
    drivers name theirs;
  * a command bit that has not dropped after 1 ms is cleared and reported
    with dev_err_ratelimited() every time. The first revision instead
    switched that register to the vendor's write-wait-clear sequence for
    good (a cmd_sticky bitmap, and <linux/delay.h> for its udelay). On
    a test kernel that path never ran: every bit cleared inside 1 ms;
  * sun60i_iommu_invalidate() no longer sends the range commands
    (TLB +0x044/+0x048/+0x054, PTW cache +0x068/+0x070/+0x06c). It runs
    sun60i_iommu_flush_all(): +0x030 = 3 (macro TLB and PTW cache), then
    +0x18 of every master with a user, in that order. +0x054's done bit
    waits on the VE's memory port and never drops while the VE is idle,
    its default state since
    `soc-sunxi-cedar-ve-carry-the-vendor-video-engine-interface`; with it
    every IOMMU1 invalidation timed out, three per boot and one per fault,
    1 ms each with interrupts off. The poll and the report stay: every
    command the driver still sends drops at the first read (under
    UNVERIFIED below);
  * a second defect: when a fault status survived three handler runs,
    the handler switched fault interrupts off and left the faulting master
    stopped -- the display went black until reboot. A first
    fix reset the masters once more after switching interrupts off; that
    was provoked on a test kernel and failed the same way (below). The
    give-up is gone: every handler run clears the status and resets the
    masters involved, as sun50i-iommu does (sun60i_iommu_reset_masters());
  * the probe line ("version, ENABLE, TTB") and the "translating",
    "disabled", "master N translating" and "master N in bypass" lines are
    dev_dbg (were dev_info), and the translating line no longer reads TTB
    back. No diagnostic line is deleted, only its level: the proof of this
    revision switched them on with dyndbg on the test kernel's command line
    (HARDWARE-PROVEN below); diagnostics stay on until the hardware
    proves the fix, and dyndbg keeps them reachable.
    The probe line is dev_dbg in the series' driver too;
  * probe error strings read "failed to ..." and "unknown version %#x";
  * Kconfig: `depends on 64BIT` (a 32-bit phys_addr_t would break the
    `<< 32` in sun60i_ent_addr() and the 34-bit aperture constant under
    COMPILE_TEST) and a longer help text;
  * comments carry no board names and no repo paths. A register or bit
    whose meaning comes from the vendor driver alone is marked "(BSP)" --
    what the first revision marked [V]. The per-register silicon record
    that its [S]/[W] marks pointed at is below.

The binding is the one of that series (property order, description,
example indentation) except for two lines: the maintainers entry (that
series carries a placeholder) and the example's clock and reset numbers,
90-93 and 33, which are this tree's CCU ids (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-iommu-gates-and-resets`). The upstream A733 CCU
series numbers the same gates 56, 57, 58, 73 and the reset 8.

WHAT THE HARDWARE IS -- and where each fact comes from

The A733 UM V0.92 chapter 10 is one page of features and has no register
list: two IOMMUs, IOMMU0 masters USB0_USB1, CSI, ISP,
VE_ENC; IOMMU1 masters VE_MBUS0, VE_MBUS1, DE0, DI, G2D, EINK; a 16 GiB address
space per IOMMU; at most 15 masters; per-master bypass, prefetch and
interrupt handling; full flush and local invalidation.

Everything below that is silicon. Read on the 8 GiB board (2026-09-15, our kernel with
no IOMMU driver, and the vendor system translating for the display engine)
and on a second board under a vendor-derived driver (2026-09-17):

  * VERSION +0x000 reads 0x16 on all four blocks. The driver refuses any
    other value: this is the only version the layout was read at.
  * RESET +0x010 reads 0x00037fff at reset; that bit m holds master m in
    reset when 0 is (BSP).
  * ENABLE +0x014: 0 at reset, 1 while translating.
  * TTB +0x020 = pa(L1) >> 14, and the page table itself, are PROVEN BY A
    WALK: every one of the display engine's 118 RCQ heads and every page of
    their buffers translated, and 68 buffers equal the DE registers they feed
    word for word, with tables and pages above 4 GiB. Format:
    34-bit IOVA, L1 index IOVA[33:22] (4096 entries, 16 KiB), L2 index
    IOVA[21:12] (1024 entries); 32-bit entries whose bits 31:12 are the
    address's bits 31:12 and bits 9:8 its bits 33:32; DTE valid = bits 1:0
    == 01, PTE valid = bit 1.
  * Per-master blocks, 0x200 wide from +0x400: four on IOMMU0, six on
    IOMMU1 -- the UM's counts. +0x00 reads 1 in every implemented block and 0
    beyond; attach refuses a master whose block reads 0.
  * BYP +0x04 of a master: 1 at reset, 0 on exactly the translating masters.
    So the identity domain is the reset state and costs nothing.
  * Read while translating, and written by this driver to the same values:
    TLB prefetch +0x02c = 0 (reset 0x00030000; the vendor writes 0 because
    its DT parser never reads the platform value, see 9. below), TLB and
    PTW invalidation mode +0x040 / +0x060 = 1, INT_ENABLE +0x080 = 3,
    per-master interrupt enable +0x30 = 1. The range registers +0x044/+0x048
    held page numbers and +0x068/+0x070 4 MiB numbers of the last range the
    vendor invalidated. This driver no longer writes them (see the flush change above).
  * Per-master TLB flush +0x18 reads 0 on one board after a vendor write of
    1 that was never undone.

⚠ UNVERIFIED, AND WHAT THE DRIVER DOES ABOUT IT. From the vendor driver
alone (radxa/allwinner-bsp dd2a41a2, sunxi-iommu-v2.c,
sunxi-iommu-pgtable.[ch]), marked (BSP) in the source:

  * whether a flush command bit (+0x030, per-master +0x18) dropping also
    means the operation has completed, and what +0x030's two bits flush.
    The vendor writes 1, waits 10 us and writes 0. The driver sets the bit
    and polls 1 ms; if it does not drop it clears it and logs "command %#x
    at %#x timed out", ratelimited. Under the first revision all four
    command bits dropped within 1 ms (a tree without
    `soc-sunxi-cedar-ve-carry-the-vendor-video-engine-interface` and `arm64-dts-a733-ve-decoder-and-encoder-nodes`).
    ⚠ A later measurement (8 GB board, 2026-09-22, test kernel, devmem and the
    DP sink CRC): the range command +0x054 does NOT drop while the VE is
    idle -- on both IOMMUs, IOMMU0 at ENABLE 0 included. Its done bit waits
    on the VE's MBUS matrix port gate alone (MBUS_MAT_CLK_GATING bit 14 for
    IOMMU1, bit 12 for IOMMU0); the VE power domains stay on. The
    invalidation itself takes effect (the sink CRC changed), only the
    completion never shows. +0x030, +0x06c and m2's +0x18 drop by the first
    read, about 3.5 us, VE idle or not. A stale entry that master 2 also
    held in its micro TLB went away only with +0x030 followed by m2's
    +0x18 (2 of 2); +0x030 alone, +0x18 alone and +0x18 then +0x030 left
    it. So the driver flushes, in that order, and does not use +0x054.
    The same effect through the driver's own path, and m2's +0x18 with the
    display off (DE0 reset asserted, clocks gated), which every sync now
    writes: HARDWARE-PROVEN below;
  * whether the END of an invalidation range is inclusive: moot since
    the range registers are no longer written.
  * most of the interrupt block. One L1 fault has been provoked on silicon
    (2026-09-18): INT_STA bit 16, L1PG_INT names the faulting master,
    INT_ERR_ADDR_L1 holds the IOVA, INT_STA reads clear after the handler;
    L1PG_INT keeps its bit and no write that clears it is known. The
    permission bits 14:0, L2 bit 17, the error data format and the prefetch
    flag bit 31 are (BSP). Every handler run clears the status and resets
    the masters involved, as sun50i-iommu does; a master that faults
    continuously interrupts once per fault and runs on once its address is
    mapped. The give-up this driver had -- fault interrupts off after
    three runs whose status survived the clear, the masters reset once
    more -- stopped such a master for good. Provoked on a test kernel with
    a debug patch (not carried here; called 9991 below) with fault_delay_us = 4000
    (slowing the handler to what it was on the test kernel where the
    display went black):
    one "status 0x10000 did not clear" line, INT_ENABLE 0, m2 INT_ENABLE 0,
    and the display black (sink CRC 0, TCON underflow) after the layer
    address was restored, until master 2 was reset by hand. So it was
    removed; the same run on a test kernel leaves the display running
    (HARDWARE-PROVEN below). The cost: a master that never stops faulting
    keeps the CPU in this handler once per fault, ratelimited in its
    report -- 52 runs of 20 ms each over the 3 s of that test.
  * the permission bits PTE bit 2 (R) and bit 3 (W): every walked entry had
    both set, so a direction-restricted mapping is untested.
  * the vendor's second meaning of entry addresses below 0x40000000 (CPU
    addresses from 16 GiB up, iommu_phy_to_cpu_phy()). The UM does not
    describe it; RAM here lies in [0x40000000, 9 GiB). The driver refuses to
    map anything outside [0x40000000, 16 GiB) rather than encode it on a
    guess.
  * that the master blocks sit in their master's power domain (the vendor DT
    gives each master node a power-domains entry). Not measured, and not
    what the driver relies on: what an access to a master block needs on
    this silicon is the master's MBUS port gate, which the driver
    holds while the master has a user (REVISED 2026-09-24 below). The VE
    power domains read on in every state measured, so no block has
    been seen behind a domain that is off.

DESIGN NOTES THAT LEFT THE CODE WITH THE REVISION

  * The reset is only ever de-asserted. `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-iommu-gates-and-resets` makes the eight gates
    CLK_IS_CRITICAL and the reset reads de-asserted at boot; a measurement showed
    that a gated IOMMU0 still passes its bypassed USB master's traffic.
  * builtin_platform_driver(), not builtin_platform_driver_probe(): the
    latter probes once, and the CCU and reset providers may not be there
    yet. A deferred IOMMU defers its clients, so a one-shot probe that lost
    the race would leave the display engine unbound.

WHY NOT sun50i-iommu.c

It implements the H6 generation, and three things differ, each read off its
source against this silicon: its ENABLE, BYPASS and TTB at 0x20/0x30/0x50
read zero here (0x20 is this block's TTB); its entries carry 32-bit
addresses (SUN50I_PTE_PAGE_ADDRESS_MASK GENMASK(31, 12)), which cannot
reach a page above 4 GiB -- the reason this driver exists; and it has one
bypass register for masters 0-5 of one block, where this SoC has per-master
blocks on two IOMMUs. What is taken from it is the 6.18 shape: identity
domain, domain_alloc_paging, map_pages/unmap_pages, gather-based
iotlb_sync, report_iommu_fault(). Its GFP_DMA32 table allocation is an H6
constraint and is not taken.

WHERE THE VENDOR DRIVER DIFFERS, AND WHAT THIS ONE DOES

Read end to end (sunxi-iommu-v2.c 2258 lines, sunxi-iommu-pgtable.c 505,
sunxi-iommu-pgtable.h 150, sunxi-iommu.h 91):

  1. One global domain and one group for both IOMMUs: domain_alloc returns
     the same domain to every caller, and every global register is written
     to both blocks with the read-back comparison left as "TBD" (the
     READ_EQUAL_REG macro). Here: one DT node per IOMMU, one group per
     IOMMU (generic_single_device_group), a fresh domain per allocation.
     The group per IOMMU holds for this patch only: later in the series,
     iommu-sun60i-one-page-table-for-both-instances gives the
     masters of both instances one group and one table, which both TTBs
     name, because the closed VE user space maps every buffer through the
     decoder's IOMMU. A domain is still allocated per request there, and
     each block is still written and enabled on its own.
  2. attach_dev and detach_dev are empty; translation is switched on per
     master from a second DT cell in probe_finalize, whatever domain the core
     attaches. There is no identity domain, so the core's view and BYP can
     disagree. Here: attach sets BYP = 0, the identity domain sets BYP = 1,
     per master with a user count, and #iommu-cells is 1.
  3. sunxi_iommu_map() ignores the return of the L2 fill, which overwrites a
     live entry with only a WARN_ON. Here: -EBUSY, nothing overwritten.
  4. The L2 fill computes entry i as `pent_val + SPAGE_SIZE * i` in a u32. A
     contiguous run across a 4 GiB boundary wraps bits 31:12 without
     carrying into bits 9:8: 0x0_FFFF_F000 plus one page becomes entry
     address 0, which its own remapping then reads as 16 GiB. Here: every
     entry is built from its own full physical address.
  5. Unmap (versions >= 0x14, i.e. this one) invalidates the TLB and PTW
     cache BEFORE clearing the entries, and iotlb_sync returns early for
     those versions, so a walk in between caches the stale translation
     again. Here: clear first, invalidate in iotlb_sync.
  6. Unmapping a whole 4 MiB frees its L2 table while the IOMMU may still
     hold the L1 entry in its PTW cache. Here: L2 tables live until the
     domain is freed, so no walk can reach a freed table.
  7. The interrupt handler returns IRQ_HANDLED without reading a status
     first; decodes one cause per interrupt through an else-if chain that
     checks permission bits 0-6 only (up to 15 masters exist); reports to a
     private callback table, not report_iommu_fault(). Here: IRQ_NONE when
     no status bit is set, every cause in one pass, report_iommu_fault(),
     and a guard for a status that does not clear.
  8. After a fault it pulses the per-master reset and writes 0xffffffff back,
     without restoring anything a master reset may clear. Here: the saved
     reset value is written back, and BYP and interrupt enable of the reset
     masters are re-applied.
  9. The DT carries tlb_prefetch = <0x3007f>; the parser never reads it, so
     0 is written. Both boards read 0 while translating. Here: 0 is written
     on purpose and said to be the only value seen translating.
 10. sunxi_iommu_get_idx_by_name() returns the index of the first master
     whose name does NOT match (strcasecmp() used as a boolean). Not used
     here; named because it is exported.
 11. It pulses the range invalidation (write 1, udelay(10), write 0) and
     never reads whether it completed. On this silicon the done bit never
     drops while the VE is idle, so no completion is ever seen. Here:
     +0x054 is not used; a full flush, whose command bits drop, is.

Page tables are DMA-mapped for their lifetime against the IOMMU device (a
34-bit mask, and a mapping that is not the physical address is refused), so
the cache syncs act on a real mapping, as in sun50i-iommu.

What the vendor has right, and this driver keeps because silicon agrees:
the register offsets listed under WHAT THE HARDWARE IS, TTB = pa >> 14, and
the V2 entry format.

A523 VALUE REJECTED: none taken. Mainline's binding files
allwinner,sun55i-a523-iommu under the allwinner,sun50i-h616-iommu fallback,
i.e. the H6 layout, which is the layout shown above not to be this one.

