REVISED 2026-09-24: the master's MBUS port gate

A master's register block inside the IOMMU (0x400 + 0x200*m) does not
answer while that master's MBUS matrix port is gated; the access stalls the
bus. Measured for the invalidation acknowledge on the 8 GB board
(2026-09-22): the per-master range command completes
only with the master's port gate set -- MBUS_MAT_CLK_GATING bit 14 for the
decoder (IOMMU1 m0/m1), bit 12 for the encoder (IOMMU0 m3) -- and times out
with every other VE clock, gate and reset of that master set alone. A
plain read of a VE master's block stalled the board the same way (a
register dump, 2026-09-22), and so does the flush this driver sends on
every unmap (the control below). Nothing in the first two revisions made
sure of it:

  * attach_dev() read AUTO_GATING of every fwspec id to refuse a master
    that does not exist. attach runs from dma_configure, before the
    consumer's probe (drivers/base/dd.c:700-704 before :719), with the
    port in whatever state it was left; at boot it was still on (the
    control attached without a stall), but nothing guarantees that. That
    read is gone: of_xlate() bounds the id to the 15 slots of the user
    manual, and a slot without a master reads 0 and ignores writes (read
    on both instances);
  * the IOMMU now takes each gated master's port as a named clock,
    "master0" .. "master14" (binding: optional, after the four it had),
    prepares it at probe and enables it in sun60i_iommu_master_translate()
    before the first block access, disabling it in
    sun60i_iommu_master_bypass() after the last. The gate is held for as
    long as the master has a user, so flush_all() and the fault handler
    only ever touch a block whose port is on, and the block never sees its
    port gated while it holds this driver's state (whether it would keep
    it is an open question, not measured). Precedent: exynos-iommu's "master" clock
    (drivers/iommu/exynos-iommu.c:692-722);
  * the fault handler reads and resets only masters with a user. A stale
    bit of L1PG_INT/L2PG_INT could name a master in bypass whose
    port is gated;
  * probe enables the four named IOMMU clocks instead of every clock the
    node lists, which would have held every master's port gate on from
    boot;
  * attach undoes the masters it switched on if one of them fails.

